#!/usr/bin/env python3
#
# gen-steamrpc -- derive the cross-process marshal descriptors for the
# lsteamclient bridge from Proton's own generated params structs.
#
# WHAT THIS EXISTS FOR.  Proton's lsteamclient already reduces every Steamworks
# method to a flat `struct <iface>_<version>_<method>_params` handed across a
# PE<->unix boundary that shares an address space, so a pointer inside a params
# struct needs no marshalling at all there.  This port stretches that boundary
# across a PROCESS: the unix half runs as an x86-64 Linux helper under FEX,
# because the real steamclient.so is x86-64 SysV and native Wine here is
# ppc64le.  Every pointer inside a params struct therefore has to be copied,
# and copying needs a size and a direction, which Proton's structs do not
# carry.
#
# Rather than hand-classify ~6500 methods, this reads the generated header and
# classifies each pointer field by its C type and, where the type alone is not
# enough (char */void */byte pointers, and typed arrays), by the NEXT field's
# name -- the same "count/len/size/num" idiom Proton's own generator asserts
# on.  Everything it cannot classify becomes a NAMED REFUSAL rather than a
# guess: a refused method returns its default value and logs its own name and
# the reason once, in the tree's sentinel style.  A wrong size here is a
# silent memory bug in someone else's game, so the classifier fails closed.
#
# The output is C, not data, so that every size is a `sizeof` and every offset
# an `offsetof` evaluated by the compiler that also compiles the params
# structs.  The generator never needs to know a struct layout.
#
# Usage:  tools/steamrpc/gen-steamrpc <proton-lsteamclient-dir> <out.c>
#
# Copyright 2026 the ppc64le port authors
